tokens.py
Token definitions for the EasyRegEx DSL.

Every token produced by the lexer carries: which kind of token it is
(TokenType), the exact text matched, and where it was found in the
source file (line, column) so later phases and error messages can point
back to the original .rx file.


lexer.py
Lexical analyzer for the EasyRegEx DSL.

Converts raw .rx source text into a flat list of Token objects that the
parser consumes. Implemented with a single master regular expression
built from named groups -- each source position is matched against every
candidate token pattern at once, and the first group that matches wins.

parser.py
Syntax analyzer (parser) for EasyRegEx.

A hand-written recursive-descent parser: there is one function per
grammar rule, and the call structure of those functions mirrors the
grammar itself. It consumes the token stream produced by the lexer and
builds an Abstract Syntax Tree.

Grammar (BNF):

    program      ::= { pattern_def | test_block }
    pattern_def  ::= "pattern" IDENT ":" body
    body         ::= { [ "then" ] term }
    term         ::= quantified | atom
    quantified   ::= "one" "or" "more" atom
                   | "zero" "or" "more" atom
                   | "optional" atom
                   | "exactly" NUMBER atom
                   | NUMBER "to" NUMBER atom
    atom         ::= STRING
                   | CLASS
                   | "use" IDENT
                   | "(" alternative { "or" alternative } ")"
    alternative  ::= term { [ "then" ] term }
    test_block   ::= "test" IDENT ":" { test_case }
    test_case    ::= ( "match" | "reject" ) STRING

A pattern body runs until the next "pattern" or "test" keyword, or the
end of the file. Newlines separate rules but are otherwise insignificant,
and the keyword "then" is optional sugar that improves readability.

semantic.py
Semantic analyzer for EasyRegEx.

A program can be grammatically correct and still be meaningless. This
phase walks the AST and checks the things the grammar cannot express:

  1. Duplicate pattern names.
  2. References (`use X`) to patterns that were never defined.
  3. Circular references, direct (a uses a) or indirect (a uses b uses a).
     These must be rejected because code generation inlines references,
     so a cycle would never terminate.
  4. Invalid quantifier ranges, such as `5 to 2` or `exactly 0`.
  5. Test blocks that refer to an undefined pattern.

All errors found are collected rather than raised one at a time, so the
user sees every problem in a single run, the way a real compiler reports
diagnostics.


ast_nodes.py
Abstract Syntax Tree node types for EasyRegEx.

The AST is the intermediate representation of the compiler: the parser
builds it from the token stream, the semantic analyzer validates it, the
optimizer simplifies it, and the code generator walks it to emit a
regular expression.

Node hierarchy:

    Program
      +-- PatternDef        pattern email: ...
      |     +-- Sequence    the body: one item after another
      |           +-- Literal        "@"
      |           +-- CharClass      digit
      |           +-- Reference      use domain
      |           +-- Group          ("com" or "org")
      |           +-- Quantified     one or more <inner>
      +-- TestBlock         test email: ...
            +-- TestCase    match "a@b.com"

codegen.py
Code generator for EasyRegEx.

Walks the validated AST and emits a standard regular expression, using
Python's `re` syntax as the target language. Each node type has one
translation rule, and fragments are composed bottom-up into the final
expression.

Translation rules:

    Literal("a.b")        ->  a\\.b          (metacharacters escaped)
    CharClass(digit)      ->  [0-9]
    CharClass(letter)     ->  [a-zA-Z]
    Group(A, B)           ->  (?:A|B)
    Quantified(X, 1, inf) ->  X+
    Quantified(X, 0, inf) ->  X*
    Quantified(X, 0, 1)   ->  X?
    Quantified(X, n, n)   ->  X{n}
    Quantified(X, n, m)   ->  X{n,m}
    Reference(name)       ->  the generated regex of that pattern, inlined
    Sequence(A, B, C)     ->  ABC

The whole pattern is anchored with ^ and $ so that a match means the
entire input string conforms, which is what a validation rule implies.

An important detail is grouping. A quantifier binds to a single element,
so a multi-character fragment must be wrapped in a non-capturing group
before a quantifier is attached: "ab" repeated is (?:ab)+, not ab+, which
would only repeat the b. Each translation therefore returns both the
regex text and whether that text is atomic (safe to quantify as-is).

optimizer.py
Optimizer for EasyRegEx.

Runs over the AST after semantic analysis and before code generation,
rewriting it into an equivalent but simpler tree. A smaller tree produces
a shorter regular expression without changing which strings it matches.

Optimizations implemented:

  1. Constant folding of literals
        "a" "b" "c"            ->  "abc"
     Adjacent literals in a sequence are merged into one.

  2. Sequence flattening
        Sequence[Sequence[a, b], c]  ->  Sequence[a, b, c]

  3. Redundant group elimination
        (X)                    ->  X
     A group with only one alternative is not a choice at all.

  4. Redundant quantifier elimination
        exactly 1 X            ->  X

  5. Single-element sequence unwrapping
        Sequence[X]            ->  X

Each pass records how many rewrites it performed, so the effect of
optimization can be reported and measured.

testrunner.py
Test runner for EasyRegEx.

The final stage of the pipeline. It takes the regular expressions
produced by the code generator and the expectations declared in the
program's `test` blocks, runs each sample string against the matching
pattern, and reports whether the result was what the author said it
should be.

This is what turns the compiler from something that merely produces
output into something that can prove its output is correct.

errors.py
Error types shared by every phase of the EasyRegEx compiler.

All compiler errors carry the line and column at which the problem was
found, so the message can point the user back to the exact position in
the .rx source file.

compiler.py
Compiler driver for EasyRegEx.

Runs the phases in order and collects the artefact each one produces, so
that the command-line interface (and any other front end) can inspect
the result of any individual stage.

main.py
Command-line interface for the EasyRegEx compiler.

Usage:
    python main.py <file.rx> [options]

Options:
    --tokens        show the token stream produced by the lexer
    --ast           show the abstract syntax tree built by the parser
    --symbols       show the symbol table built by the semantic analyzer
    --regex         show the generated regular expressions (default)
    --test          run the test cases declared in the file
    --all           show every stage of the pipeline
    --no-optimize   skip the optimizer, to compare its effect
    --match STR     test one string against every generated pattern

With no options, the generated regular expressions are printed and any
declared test cases are run.




app.py
Optional web interface for the EasyRegEx compiler.

Run with:
    pip install flask
    python app.py

then open http://127.0.0.1:5000 in a browser.

The page lets you type EasyRegEx source on the left and see every stage
of the compiler on the right: the token stream, the abstract syntax tree,
the generated regular expression, and the result of the declared test
cases. A separate box lets you try any string against the compiled
patterns without editing the source.

This is a thin front end: all the work is done by the same compiler
package that the command-line tool uses.


